  "motto": [
   "Knit in secret.",
   "Seated at the table."
  ],
  "lead": [
   "The Bible says every human being is made “in the image of God”. That means each person is made to reflect God and to belong to Him, and so is never just a body to be used or a cost to be weighed. This study teaches one reading habit. When a passage tells you how to treat a person, ask who is in the room who cannot speak for themselves: the unborn child, the disabled man, the poor stranger, the dying patient."
  ],
  "frame": [
   "Most of us agree that people matter. The hard part is deciding which people we notice. The passages in this study keep pointing at the ones who are easy to overlook. One was not yet born. One was lame in both feet and living in a town far from the palace. One came to church in shabby clothes. One was a foreigner in a lawsuit with no one to speak for him. Scripture does not let any of them drop out of sight."
  ],
  "selection": [
   "This is a selection, not a census. It walks five passages that this site’s other studies do not walk: ",
   {
    "r": "PSA.139.13-16",
    "t": "Psalm 139:13–16"
   },
   ", ",
   {
    "r": "PSA.139.2",
    "t": "2"
   },
   " Samuel 9, ",
   {
    "r": "JAS.2.1-9",
    "t": "James 2:1–9"
   },
   ", ",
   {
    "r": "EXO.23.6-9",
    "t": "Exodus 23:6–9"
   },
   " and ",
   {
    "r": "PRO.31.8-9",
    "t": "Proverbs 31:8–9"
   },
   ". Other passages are quoted briefly and linked to the study that reads them in full. The fuller teaching on what the image of God is belongs to ",
   {
    "s": "../humanity-sin-and-renewal-directions/",
    "t": "Humanity, sin and renewal"
   },
   ". This study is not medical, legal or professional advice. Where it touches a decision about treatment, a pregnancy or a fertility clinic, it gives Scripture and the church’s teaching, and it sends you to the people who can advise you."
  ],
  "close": {
   "title": "Who is in the room?",
   "way": [
    "Here is the habit to carry to the next passage. When Scripture tells you how to treat people, look around the scene for the one who cannot speak: the child in the womb, the man from Lo-debar, the poor man asked to stand, the foreigner in the lawsuit, the patient at the bedside. Then ask what the passage asks you to do for them. The answer will often be small and costly: a seat, a meal, a word spoken in the right room, a refusal to be bought."
   ],
   "close": [
    "David asked, “Is there anyone left?” and went looking. God knit each of us in secret before anyone else could see us, and He saw. If you have never trusted Christ, start there. The God who made you in His image sent His Son to die and rise for people who could give Him nothing back. Turn to Him and trust Him as Lord. Then go and set a place at the table."
   ],
   "question": [
    "Who is the person in your life it would be easiest to stop seeing, and what is one thing you will do this week so that they are seen?"
   ],
   "again": [
    "Read the passage again before you answer."
   ],
   "passage": "2SA.9"
  }
